{
  "id": "perfekt",
  "name": "Past simple",
  "level": "A2",
  "subtitle": "Use the past simple for finished actions in the past: I worked yesterday. Regular verbs add -ed. Many common verbs are irregular: go → went. In negatives and questions, use did / didn't + the base verb.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "-ed",
    "irregular",
    "did / didn't"
  ],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-a2 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "I / she / they …"
      },
      {
        "text": "verb + -ed / irregular form",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "…"
      },
      {
        "text": "yesterday · last week · ago",
        "variant": "prep"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "I <span class=\"vp-verb\">worked</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">yesterday</span>. · We <span class=\"vp-verb\">went</span> to London <span class=\"vp-prep\">last week</span>. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Did</span> you <span class=\"vp-verb\">see</span> it?",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "Use the <strong>past simple</strong> for an action that is finished, at a time in the past: <em>I called Mum yesterday. We moved here in 2019.</em> The form is the same for every person: <em>I / you / he / we / they worked.</em>",
        "tag": "Use"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Regular verbs</strong> add <em>-ed</em>: <em>work → worked, watch → watched</em>. Verb ends in <em>-e</em>: add <em>-d</em> (<em>live → lived</em>). One short vowel + one consonant: double it (<em>stop → stopped</em>). Consonant + <em>y</em>: <em>y → ied</em> (<em>study → studied</em>). Vowel + <em>y</em>: just <em>-ed</em> (<em>play → played</em>).",
        "tag": "Regular: -ed"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Irregular verbs</strong> have their own past form. You have to learn them: <em>go → went, have → had, see → saw, make → made, get → got, take → took, come → came</em>. The form is the same for all persons: <em>I went, she went, they went.</em>",
        "tag": "Irregular"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Negative</strong>: <em>didn't</em> + base verb: <em>I didn't go. She didn't see it.</em> <strong>Question</strong>: <em>Did</em> + subject + base verb: <em>Did you go? What did she see?</em> Short answers: <em>Yes, I did. / No, I didn't.</em> After <em>did</em> and <em>didn't</em>, the verb does <em>not</em> change.",
        "tag": "did / didn't"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Time words</strong> show that the action is finished: <em>yesterday, last night, last week, last year, two days ago, in 2019, in June.</em> <em>Ago</em> comes after the time: <em>three years ago</em>.",
        "tag": "Time words"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Be</strong> is irregular: <em>I / he / she / it was</em>, <em>you / we / they were</em>. Negative: <em>wasn't / weren't</em>. Question: <em>Was he late? Were you at home?</em> We do not use <em>did</em> with be.",
        "tag": "was / were"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>I goed to the shop.</em> → ✓ <em>I went to the shop.</em> · ✗ <em>She didn't went home.</em> → ✓ <em>She didn't go home.</em> · ✗ <em>Did you saw the film?</em> → ✓ <em>Did you see the film?</em> · ✗ <em>Yesterday I go to work.</em> → ✓ <em>Yesterday I went to work.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "How do we say <em>-ed</em>? After <em>t</em> or <em>d</em> it is an extra syllable: <em>wanted /ɪd/</em>. After other voiceless sounds it is /t/: <em>worked, stopped</em>. After voiced sounds it is /d/: <em>lived, played</em>. For more negatives, see <a href=\"/negation\">Negatives</a>. To compare with now, see <a href=\"/praesens\">Present simple</a> and <a href=\"/konjugation\">Present continuous</a>.",
